#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# singular_values

#% text
# `singular_values(m)` gives the nonzero singular values of the matrix `m`, in decreasing order.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The singular values are the square roots of the eigenvalues of `dot(conjugate_transpose(m), m)`. Exact matrices give exact values where the eigenvalues are exact.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
singular_values([[1.0, 2.0], [3.0, 4.0]])
#% output
#= [5.4649857042190435, 0.3659661906262578]

#% cell
singular_values([[3, 0], [0, 4]])
#% output
#= [4, 3]

#% cell
singular_values([[1.0, 1.0], [1.0, 1.0]])
#% output
#= [1.9999999999999998]

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# The condition number of a matrix in the 2-norm:

#% cell
s = singular_values([[1.0, 2.0], [2.0, 4.001]]); first(s)/last(s)
#% output
#= 25008.00095999726

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The largest singular value is the 2-norm:

#% cell
[first(singular_values([[1.0, 2.0], [3.0, 4.0]])), norm([[1.0, 2.0], [3.0, 4.0]])]
#% output
#= [5.4649857042190435, 5.4649857042190435]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `svd`, `norm`, `rank`, `eigenvalues`
